01:36:03.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/SWx9qKLNwPkn9EKqsKibw","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/SWx9qKLNwPkn9EKqsKibw"}},{"id":"9305","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"3031","description":"","caption":"Steve and Jeff","poi_name":"Huss street  wave","subject":"","author":"","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/9305","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/9305"}},{"id":"9299","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"3031","description":"","caption":"OH, Sandusky River, Huss Street Wave","poi_name":"Huss street wave","subject":"Steve,Jeff and Bernie","author":"","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/9299","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/9299"}},{"id":"1341","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"3031","description":"","caption":"Steve","poi_name":"","subject":"Steve","author":"Jeff","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/1341","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/1341"}},{"id":"3262","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"3032","description":"","caption":"Jeff in the eddy","poi_name":"Huss street  wave","subject":"Steve with Jeff waiting in  the eddy","author":"Dein Higgins","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/3262","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/3262"}},{"id":"1844","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"3031","description":"","caption":"High water","poi_name":"","subject":"Brent Koontz","author":"Dan Koontz","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/1844","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/1844"}},{"id":"1779","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"3033","description":"","caption":"Where'd he go?","poi_name":"","subject":"Brent Koontz","author":"Jeff Totten","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/1779","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/1779"}},{"id":"1780","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"3033","description":"","caption":"Jay Observing","poi_name":"","subject":"Brent Koontz","author":"Jeff Totten","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/1780","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/1780"}},{"id":"15352","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"3034","description":"","caption":"Huss street wave","poi_name":"Huss street wave","subject":"Tim Staley","author":"?","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/15352","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/15352"}},{"id":"3261","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"3032","description":"","caption":"Huss street wave","poi_name":"Huss street wave","subject":"Steve","author":"Dein Higgins","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/3261","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/3261"}}],"detail":{"id":"1477","permitUrl":null,"permitInfo":null,"description":"**About the Class III Rating**\n\nThe Class III rating for this section is primarily due to the potential for swamping open canoes in the larger features—particularly at Huss Street. It's worth noting that the rapids here don’t require much maneuvering. Any finesse tends to come from surfing opportunities rather than technical navigation.\n\nAt higher water levels, swims can be long and hazardous. The river begins to flood its banks, which limits your ability to exit safely. In these conditions, strainers and trees along the edge become serious hazards, making self-rescue or assisting others more difficult.\n\n**Note:** Downstream of the Rt. 38 bridge, there is a single river-wide wave about a mile downstream—and that’s the only feature of note. This rapid is not included in the details below because running it would require taking out at a canoe livery, which has been closed since approximately 2018.\n\nThe [Sandusky River by the Haunted Hydro](http://www.americanwhitewater.org/content/River/view/river-detail/1478/) is only 15 minutes away. It can be a fun surf area at the right levels\n\nThe [Maumee River](http://www.americanwhitewater.org/content/River/view/river-detail/1472/) is another local run 45 minutes away. It is a lot easier than this section, but it can be fun.","averageGradient":"12","maxGradient":"18","length":"3.2","correlations":[{"reachID":"1477","isPrimary":true,"correlationDetails":{"metric":"cfs","belowRecommendedRangeComment":"You can still paddle the river at this level, but expect to scrape the bottom for much of the way.","lowRunnableRangeComment":"The water level is low, and aside from Huss Street, there’s not much action on the river right now.","mediumRunnableRangeComment":"Fireball is surfable at this range.","highRunnableRangeComment":"At lower flows, consider using Huss Street as a park-and-play feature. Above 5,000 CFS, the river transforms into a big water run. Aside from Huss Street, there’s little technical challenge—just fast-moving, powerful water offering a fun and thrilling high-volume experience.","aboveRecommendedRangeComment":"The river is currently in flood stage. For your safety and in accordance with local regulations, it is illegal to enter the water at this time. Please respect closures and wait for conditions to improve before paddling.","lowRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":"II","highRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":"IIIplus","beginLowRunnable":"1000","beginMediumRunnable":"2000","beginHighRunnable":"5000","endHighRunnable":"11000"},"gaugeInfo":{"name":"Sandusky River a